00:09:36,700 --> 00:09:39,380
Now that's great.

170
00:09:39,380 --> 00:09:42,515
That got us a virtual machine.

171
00:09:42,515 --> 00:09:45,170
Now what can we do
inside of that?

172
00:09:45,170 --> 00:09:49,685
The second piece of information
is about containers.

173
00:09:49,685 --> 00:09:51,500
We're going to be using
containers and we're going to be

174
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:53,840
using Docker in particular.

175
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:58,535
If you're not familiar
with what containers are,

176
00:09:58,535 --> 00:10:01,145
it's not a problem.
They're real simple.

177
00:10:01,145 --> 00:10:05,180
Let me give you a quick
overview of them.

178
00:10:05,180 --> 00:10:07,640
To just understand the problem

179
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:09,620
that containers were solving,

180
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:12,410
let's start with bare metal.

181
00:10:12,410 --> 00:10:16,730
What is a machine or
what is the server?

182
00:10:16,730 --> 00:10:21,425
We want to be able to
run in bare metal.

183
00:10:21,425 --> 00:10:23,795
We run applications
directly on server.

184
00:10:23,795 --> 00:10:26,045
Down at the bottom,
we have our hardware,

185
00:10:26,045 --> 00:10:27,440
so this is the network card,

186
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:29,885
the disc, the CPU, the memory.

187
00:10:29,885 --> 00:10:32,690
On top of that, we run
an operating system

188
00:10:32,690 --> 00:10:35,630
like Windows or Linux.

189
00:10:35,630 --> 00:10:40,805
Then on top of the
operating system,

190
00:10:40,805 --> 00:10:43,670
we have all the
binaries and libraries

191
00:10:43,670 --> 00:10:47,090
, the specific distribution.

192
00:10:47,090 --> 00:10:48,980
This is like Ubuntu.

193
00:10:48,980 --> 00:10:52,700
Which one's on Linux or
Arch Linux or Windows

194
00:10:52,700 --> 00:10:57,830
7 or specific
variant of Windows?

195
00:10:57,830 --> 00:11:01,250
Then we run our
applications on top.

196
00:11:01,250 --> 00:11:03,335
Like the operating system,

197
00:11:03,335 --> 00:11:05,795
it's responsible
for CPU scheduling,

198
00:11:05,795 --> 00:11:08,465
memory management,
network stack,

199
00:11:08,465 --> 00:11:10,520
the binaries and libraries.

200
00:11:10,520 --> 00:11:14,285
These are things
like all the files,

201
00:11:14,285 --> 00:11:20,340
the package, the commands
that we can run like LS.

202
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:26,075
The challenge here
is historically,

203
00:11:26,075 --> 00:11:30,755
resource wastage, only
using part of a server.

204
00:11:30,755 --> 00:11:33,635
If we're going to run one
application on one server,

205
00:11:33,635 --> 00:11:35,790
that's going to waste resources.

206
00:11:35,790 --> 00:11:37,290
Ultimately, we want to run

207
00:11:37,290 --> 00:11:40,560
multiple applications
on each server.

208
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:45,700
But this causes some
problems, difficulties.

209
00:11:45,700 --> 00:11:50,165
Applications might need
different operating systems.

210
00:11:50,165 --> 00:11:52,955
Applications might have
variable workloads.

211
00:11:52,955 --> 00:11:55,865
They might interfere
with one another.

212
00:11:55,865 --> 00:11:58,685
They might have
conflicting dependencies

213
00:11:58,685 --> 00:12:01,580
like Application 1
might need Python 3,

214
00:12:01,580 --> 00:12:06,215
Application 2 might
need Python 2.

215
00:12:06,215 --> 00:12:08,960
Sometimes these are complex

216
00:12:08,960 --> 00:12:11,885
to resolve those dependencies.

217
00:12:11,885 --> 00:12:14,900
From that, virtualization

218
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:17,315
or virtual machines
were introduced,

219
00:12:17,315 --> 00:12:21,725
where we can run a software
layer known as a Hypervisor,

220
00:12:21,725 --> 00:12:24,005
that makes it possible to run

221
00:12:24,005 --> 00:12:27,125
multiple independent
operating systems.

222
00:12:27,125 --> 00:12:31,070
Now we can have an
operating system,

223
00:12:31,070 --> 00:12:34,310
let's say over here
we're running Windows,

224
00:12:34,310 --> 00:12:37,100
and over here we're
running Linux.

225
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:39,590
Or maybe is both Linux and

226
00:12:39,590 --> 00:12:42,260
we're just running different
distributions of it.

227
00:12:42,260 --> 00:12:43,970
But this provides us with

228
00:12:43,970 --> 00:12:46,790
this nice isolation and

229
00:12:46,790 --> 00:12:48,965
now I can run my
application in here,

230
00:12:48,965 --> 00:12:51,530
not have to worry
about dependencies or

231
00:12:51,530 --> 00:12:57,950
any other problems of interference
or overlap with this.

232
00:12:57,950 --> 00:13:01,190
It makes it much more simple.

233
00:13:01,190 --> 00:13:04,475
To recap that,

234
00:13:04,475 --> 00:13:06,290
challenges with bare metal,

235
00:13:06,290 --> 00:13:08,090
there was the resource usage,

236
00:13:08,090 --> 00:13:10,145
so we wanted to run
multiple applications,

237
00:13:10,145 --> 00:13:12,005
but there is challenges.

238
00:13:12,005 --> 00:13:15,380
Virtualization made it so

239
00:13:15,380 --> 00:13:18,440
that it solved all the problems

240
00:13:18,440 --> 00:13:21,470
that we just discussed
about the conflicts,

241
00:13:21,470 --> 00:13:23,780
about needing different
operating systems,

242
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:25,445
different workloads,

243
00:13:25,445 --> 00:13:27,650
conflicting dependencies
and so forth.

244
00:13:27,650 --> 00:13:29,120
Now the problem with

245
00:13:29,120 --> 00:13:33,860
virtual machines is that
it's a bit heavyweight.

246
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:38,495
There's CPU, memory disk for
an extra operating system,

247
00:13:38,495 --> 00:13:40,790
and a full set of binaries.

248
00:13:40,790 --> 00:13:43,070
There's the overhead,

249
00:13:43,070 --> 00:13:45,605
the OS traditionally assumes

250
00:13:45,605 --> 00:13:49,370
that they are directly
on the hardware.

251
00:13:49,370 --> 00:13:52,970
The CPU architecture
has improved,

252
00:13:52,970 --> 00:13:55,730
but it's still some performance

253
00:13:55,730 --> 00:13:58,410
overhead running
virtual machines.

254
00:13:59,080 --> 00:14:02,555
Then came containers,

255
00:14:02,555 --> 00:14:06,350
or the next evolution of
that concept is containers.

256
00:14:06,350 --> 00:14:10,170
What if we don't need
different operating systems?

257
00:14:10,510 --> 00:14:13,400
The solution here
is to introduce

258
00:14:13,400 --> 00:14:19,020
isolation mechanisms inside
of the operating system.

259
00:14:19,780 --> 00:14:22,490
Now the operating
system is all the same.

260
00:14:22,490 --> 00:14:25,925
Let's say we're only
running on Linux,

261
00:14:25,925 --> 00:14:30,230
but I might have a different
set of dependencies.

262
00:14:30,230 --> 00:14:32,990
I might have different
binaries and libraries and

263
00:14:32,990 --> 00:14:34,250
here we're showing two

264
00:14:34,250 --> 00:14:36,500
completely different
distributions.

265
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:38,270
But going to the example,

266
00:14:38,270 --> 00:14:42,425
maybe this is focused on
Python 3 and this is Python 2.

267
00:14:42,425 --> 00:14:43,670
Now I don't have to worry about

268
00:14:43,670 --> 00:14:47,300
resolving the conflicts
between those,

269
00:14:47,300 --> 00:14:50,285
but I could still run them both.

270
00:14:50,285 --> 00:14:51,725
And it's much lighter weight.

271
00:14:51,725 --> 00:14:55,745
It's really just the same
as running a process.

272
00:14:55,745 --> 00:14:58,355
You have extra files, of course,

273
00:14:58,355 --> 00:15:01,910
but it's a much more efficient.

274
00:15:01,910 --> 00:15:03,740
Now, this is not new technology.

275
00:15:03,740 --> 00:15:06,710
It's had a evolution

276
00:15:06,710 --> 00:15:10,955
from the early 1980s
through the early 2000s.

277
00:15:10,955 --> 00:15:13,730
Really, where we stand
is inside of the kernel,

278
00:15:13,730 --> 00:15:16,620
the two key mechanisms

279
00:15:17,080 --> 00:15:22,970
that support this are called
namespaces and cgroups.

280
00:15:22,970 --> 00:15:27,020
Namespaces control
what resources

281
00:15:27,020 --> 00:15:29,600
and namings of those resources,

282
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:31,910
or processes like the
file descriptors,

283
00:15:31,910 --> 00:15:34,205
the IP addresses, and so forth.

284
00:15:34,205 --> 00:15:37,040
And the cgroup or control group,

285
00:15:37,040 --> 00:15:41,285
groups processes and
allocates resources,

286
00:15:41,285 --> 00:15:45,605
CPU memory that the
kernel will enforce.

287
00:15:45,605 --> 00:15:48,630
With this, we can now

288
00:15:48,700 --> 00:15:52,130
have that same benefit
of the isolation,

289
00:15:52,130 --> 00:15:54,620
but in a much more
efficient manner.

290
00:15:54,620 --> 00:15:56,765
Now you might be thinking,

291
00:15:56,765 --> 00:16:00,260
and many people thought
Docker invented containers.

292
00:16:00,260 --> 00:16:04,955
And we can see they date back
well before Docker existed.

293
00:16:04,955 --> 00:16:08,060
What did they bring?
They really brought

294
00:16:08,060 --> 00:16:11,855
nice packaging around
container technology.

295
00:16:11,855 --> 00:16:13,685
They made it usable,

296
00:16:13,685 --> 00:16:17,070
which was a huge innovation.

297
00:16:17,070 --> 00:16:21,990
Let me give a quick overview
of the Docker architecture.

298
00:16:21,990 --> 00:16:26,255
What we're going to show is
three separate machines.

299
00:16:26,255 --> 00:16:28,070
They could all be on
the same machine,

300
00:16:28,070 --> 00:16:30,425
but we'll consider
them separate for now.

301
00:16:30,425 --> 00:16:32,165
Most of the time you'll have

302
00:16:32,165 --> 00:16:35,090
the client and host
be a single machine,

303
00:16:35,090 --> 00:16:41,040
and then the registry be off
on the Internet somewhere.

304
00:16:41,290 --> 00:16:46,430
On the host, you have
running containers.

305
00:16:46,430 --> 00:16:51,275
A container is a process that
runs in its own namespace,

306
00:16:51,275 --> 00:16:54,590
has its own file
directory structure,

307
00:16:54,590 --> 00:16:56,960
its own network resources,

308
00:16:56,960 --> 00:16:59,435
own file descriptors,

309
00:16:59,435 --> 00:17:02,160
and all of that.

310
00:17:02,410 --> 00:17:06,185
The next concept is images.

311
00:17:06,185 --> 00:17:09,275
An image is an immutable,

312
00:17:09,275 --> 00:17:11,060
meaning it doesn't change,

313
00:17:11,060 --> 00:17:14,165
snapshot of a file system.

314
00:17:14,165 --> 00:17:17,120
That means it's a
collection of files and

315
00:17:17,120 --> 00:17:20,285
directories such as shown here.

316
00:17:20,285 --> 00:17:23,060
It's used as the
initial collection of

317
00:17:23,060 --> 00:17:27,120
files within a
container's namespace.

318
00:17:28,230 --> 00:17:32,230
Now, docker provides

319
00:17:32,230 --> 00:17:36,100
an executable that interacts

320
00:17:36,100 --> 00:17:39,760
with the daemon to
initiate docker commands.

321
00:17:39,760 --> 00:17:42,655
You could say something
like docker run,

322
00:17:42,655 --> 00:17:46,510
which will then send a command
to this docker daemon,

323
00:17:46,510 --> 00:17:49,030
which is a process that runs on

324
00:17:49,030 --> 00:17:51,595
each machine you want
to run containers on.

325
00:17:51,595 --> 00:17:54,010
It manages the images and

326
00:17:54,010 --> 00:17:57,340
starting processes
and interfacing

327
00:17:57,340 --> 00:18:00,025
to the cgroups and namespaces.

328
00:18:00,025 --> 00:18:01,705
When you say docker run,

329
00:18:01,705 --> 00:18:04,300
it'll contact the daemon,

330
00:18:04,300 --> 00:18:09,920
get the start a container
with a particular image.

331
00:18:10,080 --> 00:18:14,260
Then the last piece
is the registry.

332
00:18:14,260 --> 00:18:18,970
The registry is a storage
for images and enables

333
00:18:18,970 --> 00:18:21,610
an interface to pull or push

334
00:18:21,610 --> 00:18:24,940
images to or from a local host.

335
00:18:24,940 --> 00:18:27,985
So docker provides a build
system where you can say,

336
00:18:27,985 --> 00:18:31,105
I want to build an image
that looks like this.

337
00:18:31,105 --> 00:18:36,535
That will make it so
that you can build it.

338
00:18:36,535 --> 00:18:38,845
It'll be able to put it
in the local image tour,

339
00:18:38,845 --> 00:18:41,515
but you can also push
it to a registry.

340
00:18:41,515 --> 00:18:44,290
Now, the most popular one

341
00:18:44,290 --> 00:18:46,930
is called docker hub
and that's where I'm

342
00:18:46,930 --> 00:18:54,080
storing some images that
we'll use for this class.

343
00:18:54,270 --> 00:18:57,370
So with all that,

344
00:18:57,370 --> 00:18:59,515
a summary of containers is

345
00:18:59,515 --> 00:19:02,590
a temporary file system
that's layered over

346
00:19:02,590 --> 00:19:04,450
an image that's fully

347
00:19:04,450 --> 00:19:09,325
writable and then disappears
when it is end of life.

348
00:19:09,325 --> 00:19:13,670
Each container has its
own network stack.

349
00:19:14,160 --> 00:19:17,995
It consists of a process group,

350
00:19:17,995 --> 00:19:22,780
which has one main process
with possible subprocess,

351
00:19:22,780 --> 00:19:26,455
and all of those exit when
the main process exits.

352
00:19:26,455 --> 00:19:29,905
We'll see this inaction plenty,

353
00:19:29,905 --> 00:19:34,105
but that was a quick overview.

354
00:19:34,105 --> 00:19:36,520
Certainly go read up end
containers if you've

355
00:19:36,520 --> 00:19:39,025
never used them before,

356
00:19:39,025 --> 00:19:43,675
but we'll get plenty of
exposure in this course.

357
00:19:43,675 --> 00:19:47,170
So that brings us
to containerlab.

